    <!-- rules page (design content.tone.rulesPage) -->
    <string name="rules_title">How to play</string>
    <string name="welcome_title">Welcome to Luban Lock</string>
    <string name="rules_1">Pick a stick by its number in this window. The ways it can go light up. Tap one and it slides.</string>
    <string name="rules_2">Take all six apart, then put them back one by one.</string>
    <string name="rules_3">Stuck? Turn on the hint. It tells you why.</string>
    <string name="why_title">Why it comes apart</string>
    <string name="why_key">The key: one stick has no cuts. The rest are cut around it.</string>
    <string name="why_notch">Notches: a cut lets another stick slide past.</string>
    <string name="why_gap">Gaps: some cells inside are empty. Nudge into them to make room.</string>
    <string name="mistakes_title">Common slips</string>
    <string name="mistake_1">From outside they all look the same. The cuts are inside. Pick each one to find who moves.</string>
    <string name="mistake_2">Sticks slide straight. No lifting, no prying sideways.</string>
    <string name="mistake_3">Sometimes you nudge one first so the next can come out.</string>
    <string name="mistake_4">Putting back is your take-apart in reverse. Last out, first in.</string>
    <string name="rules_source">Also called a Kongming lock, or a six-piece burr. It shows up in a 1698 catalogue. These three were designed on that pattern and computer-checked to come apart and go back.</string>

